            "text": "Every neighborhood should have a library of unfinished things. Not abandoned things, exactly, but things waiting for another pair of hands: a scarf with twelve rows knitted, a melody that stops before the chorus, a wooden bird whose wings remain trapped inside a block of cedar. Nothing would be overdue.\n\nYou could wander between the shelves on a rainy afternoon and borrow somebody\u2019s almost. Perhaps you would choose the half written letter to a brother, though that shelf would require special care. Perhaps you would take home a recipe missing its final ingredient and discover that it only needed lemon.\n\nThe librarians would be experts in hesitation. They would know the difference between a project that needs courage and one that needs scissors. They would never ask why you stopped. Instead, they would stamp a little card and say, \u201cSomeone else got this far. You may go a little farther.\u201d\n\nThere would be a quiet room for people who only wanted to sit beside their unfinished work without apologizing. Sunlight would cross the tables. A kettle would click itself off. Outside, buses would keep their appointments, but inside, nobody would confuse a deadline with a measure of a person\u2019s worth.\n\nAnd sometimes, very rarely, someone would return something finished. The wooden bird would have wings; the song would find its last note. We would admire it briefly, then leave an empty space on the shelf. That space would matter too: proof that an ending can be a gift to strangers."
